name 'pl-aif1-js-abstractions-spec'
title 'title'
description 'description here'

markdown <<MARKDOWN
<h1>Introducing SPEC</h1>

<p>Set functions aside for a moment, this one's about something different. When your students open Web Lab in some of the lessons, they'll find a file called <code>spec.md</code> with tags like <code>[Specify]</code> and <code>[Predict]</code> already built into it.</p>

<p> SPEC is the name for that framework, and it's actually the same instinct you've been practicing all unit: planning a prompt before writing it, judging whether AI's output matched what you asked. This just gives that instinct a name, and points you to the real file where you'll see it.</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 8px 0;">• <b>Specify:</b> say what you want before you prompt</p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 8px 0;">• <b>Predict:</b> say what you expect AI to do before it runs</p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 8px 0;">• <b>Explain:</b> say why you accepted, adapted, or rejected AI's suggestion</p>
<p style="margin: 0;">• <b>Claim:</b> say whether you could rebuild this code yourself, without AI, if it disappeared right now</p>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; color: #222222; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 20px 0;">
<p><strong>💡 Teacher tip:</strong> Claim is doing something different from the other three. Specify, Predict, and Explain are all about prompting well.</p> <p> Claim is a comprehension check: could this student actually explain or recreate this specific function on their own, not just recognize that it worked? That's the moment you find out whether a student understood what they accepted, or just liked the result.</p>
</div>

<h2>Example: spec.md</h2>

<p>Below is what your students might see when they work on levels with this.</p>

<div style="border: 1px solid #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; margin: 20px 0;">
  <div style="display: flex; background-color: #F7F7FB; border-bottom: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
    <div style="padding: 10px 18px; font-size: 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #1F1976; background-color: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #4C42CF;">spec.md</div>
    <div style="padding: 10px 18px; font-size: 14px; color: #888888; background-color: #F0F0F5;">script.js</div>
  </div>
  <div style="background-color: #1E1E2E; padding: 16px 0; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7; display: flex;">
    <div style="color: #5A5A72; text-align: right; padding: 0 14px 0 0; user-select: none; min-width: 30px;">
      1<br>2<br>3<br>4<br>5<br>6<br>7<br>8
    </div>
    <div style="color: #E4E2F8; padding-right: 20px; flex: 1;">
      Name of Button: Assign Tech<br>
      Event: click<br>
      What should happen: When a tech is assigned, the queue should change<br>
      What on the page changes: The number next to "Queue" updates<br>
      <br>
      [Specify] When this button is clicked, I want:<br>
      [Predict] I think the AI's function will:<br>
      [Return] The helper function should return:
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>SPEC isn't replacing the frameworks from earlier in this unit, Task/Context/Tone/Details, Goals/Constraints/Edge Cases. It's a layer on top of them, aimed specifically at code: not just did you write a good prompt, but do you actually understand and own what came back.</p>


Next, we will look at how to use conditionals to have your program make decisions. 

MARKDOWN
